  "translation": {
    "literal": "At daybreak I took my leave of Baidi, among the coloured clouds; a thousand li to Jiangling, and I return in a single day. The gibbons on both banks cry without cease; the light boat has already passed ten thousand folds of mountains.",
    "poetic": "I leave Baidi at dawn in rosy cloud and reach Jiangling, a thousand miles away, in a single day. From both shores the gibbons keep crying while my light boat slips past a thousand ranges."
  },
  "notes": [
    {
      "id": "history",
      "kind": "history",
      "title": "A pardon on the river",
      "body": "In 759 Li Bai was on his way into exile in Yelang when an amnesty reached him at Baidi (modern Fengjie, Chongqing). He turned back and sailed down the Yangtze to Jiangling. An older text, 水經注, counts about 1,200 li between the two places and describes the passage through the Three Gorges as fast as the wind. This poem catches the poet's relief."
    },
    {
      "id": "variants",
      "kind": "text-variant",
      "title": "Variants",
      "body": "The poem is also titled 下江陵. Some editions write 綵雲 instead of 彩雲, and some give 啼不盡 (cry without end) instead of 啼不住 (cry without stopping). The version in school texts is 啼不住."
    },
    {
      "id": "rhyme",
      "kind": "rhyme",
      "title": "Rhyme: 間 · 還 · 山",
      "body": "Lines one, two and four end in 間 (jiān), 還 (huán) and 山 (shān), all in the 刪 rhyme. Line three ends in 住 and does not rhyme. This is the usual pattern in a seven-character jueju."
    },
    {
      "id": "readings",
      "kind": "other",
      "title": "Words with more than one reading",
      "body": "朝 is read zhāo (morning) here, not cháo (dynasty, court). 重 is read chóng (layer), not zhòng (heavy). 還 is read huán (to return), not hái (still)."
    }
  ],
